{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The problem asks the target to identify evidence WITHIN A SINGLE EVALUATION that distinguishes a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. SRC_001's 'decoy' checklist items are an external design feature added before the evaluation runs, not evidence observed within the evaluation's own results. Even if decoys flag unreflective compliance, that signal concerns the panel's process adherence, not the within-evaluation evidence that the reliability gain is genuine versus coincidentally tracking the right answer. A panel could fail decoys yet still track the correct answer for spurious reasons, or pass decoys while the checklist itself is tracking a proxy; the target offers no within-evaluation evidentiary criterion separating the two.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_001"},{"attack":true,"case":"The problem demands that the answer identify evidence within a single evaluation distinguishing a genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance that happens to track the right answer. SRC_002 argues the panel is less reliable and frames checklist 'reliability' as variance reduction, but it never supplies the requested diagnostic: it offers no within-evaluation evidence criterion that would tell us, for an observed agreement pattern, whether the checklist is genuinely reliable versus merely consistently tracking the right answer for the wrong reasons. Its mechanism (additive binary checks vs multiplicative conditional logic) explains a predicted failure mode, not a within-evaluation discriminator between genuine and coincidental reliability, so it leaves the problem's evidential question unanswered.","counterexample":null,"target_alias":"SRC_002"}]}